CREATE OR REPLACE PROCEDURE swap_rows(p_id_a NUMBER, p_id_b NUMBER) IS
  l_a swap_vals.payload%TYPE;
BEGIN
  SELECT payload INTO l_a FROM swap_vals WHERE row_id = p_id_a;

  UPDATE swap_vals SET payload =
    (SELECT payload FROM swap_vals WHERE row_id = p_id_b)
   WHERE row_id = p_id_a;

  UPDATE swap_vals SET payload = l_a WHERE row_id = p_id_b;
END swap_rows;
/

BEGIN swap_rows(1, 2); COMMIT; END;
/
The temp variable holds the first row's value while the two updates exchange the payloads. Because both updates run in one transaction and are committed together, a failure before the commit rolls both back, leaving the rows unchanged — no torn swap. This is the classic pointer-swap translated to rows. A concurrent session could interleave between the SELECT and the UPDATEs, so for a strict swap under concurrency, SELECT ... FOR UPDATE both rows first or add the source rows to the WHERE of each UPDATE to re-validate. After the call, row 1 holds 'right' and row 2 holds 'left'.

