CREATE OR REPLACE PROCEDURE purge_table(p_table VARCHAR2) IS
  l_sql VARCHAR2(4000);
BEGIN
  l_sql := 'TRUNCATE TABLE ' || DBMS_ASSERT.simple_sql_name(p_table);
  EXECUTE IMMEDIATE l_sql;
  DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('Purged ' || p_table);
END purge_table;
/

BEGIN purge_table('PURGE_TARGET'); END;
/
SELECT COUNT(*) AS remaining_rows FROM purge_target;
TRUNCATE removes all rows and resets the high-water mark, freeing the space back to the segment's freelists immediately, and it cannot be rolled back — an important distinction from DELETE. DBMS_ASSERT.simple_sql_name validates the table-name argument so the dynamic DDL is not an injection vector. After the call, purge_target has zero rows (the COUNT returns 0). TRUNCATE is DDL, so it commits implicitly and does not honor a surrounding transaction. Because it resets the HWM, the freed space becomes available for new inserts, unlike a DELETE that leaves the segment at its current high-water mark.
